No. On Volta or newer, `__activemask()` inside the branch can capture only a subset of lanes 0–18, even though the full warp started converged. A caller’s mask may therefore omit lane 18, so its result is not guaranteed.

For `__shfl_sync`, every caller must have its own bit set in the mask, and every non-exited thread named in that mask must execute the same shuffle intrinsic with the same mask. The source lane must actively participate in that shuffle; reading an inactive source produces an undefined result. Width 32 makes source index 18 refer to physical lane 18—it does not add that lane to the participants. `__activemask()` is a snapshot, not a collective membership operation.

Compute the membership with a full-warp ballot before branching, then use that common mask:

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__global__ void broadcast_lane18(const float* input, float* output)
{
    const unsigned int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    const unsigned int lane = threadIdx.x & 31u;
    const float value = input[i];
    const bool participating = lane < 19u;
    const unsigned int mask = __ballot_sync(0xffffffffu, participating);

    if (participating) {
        const float result = __shfl_sync(mask, value, 18, 32);
        output[i] = result;
    }
}
```

Compile as CUDA and launch with a one-dimensional grid and a one-dimensional block whose size is a multiple of 32, for example `broadcast_lane18<<<blocks, 128>>>(input, output);`. Both device arrays must contain at least `blocks * 128` floats, and no thread may exit before the ballot. The mask is exactly `0x0007ffff`: all 19 callers rendezvous at the shuffle, including source lane 18, while lanes 19–31 never call it. Only output entries for lanes 0–18 are written; no shared memory, atomics, or additional synchronization is needed.

Use five reduction shuffles followed by one broadcast: exactly six float shuffle calls per caller. Every lane uses the fixed mask for physical lanes 0–18, and lanes without a partner shuffle from themselves but do not add.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ __forceinline__ float sum19(float value)
{
    const unsigned int linear =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned int lane = linear & 31u;
    constexpr unsigned int mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    float sum = value;
    #pragma unroll
    for (int offset = 16; offset >= 1; offset >>= 1) {
        const bool has_partner =
            lane < static_cast<unsigned int>(offset) &&
            lane + static_cast<unsigned int>(offset) < 19u;
        const int source = has_partner
            ? static_cast<int>(lane) + offset
            : static_cast<int>(lane);

        const float partner = __shfl_sync(mask, sum, source, 32);
        if (has_partner) {
            sum = sum + partner;
        }
    }

    return __shfl_sync(mask, sum, 0, 32);
}
```

Compile as CUDA, targeting `sm_70` or newer. In each participating full physical warp, exactly lanes 0–18 must call `sum19` once, with one input float each; lanes 19–31 must not call it. All nineteen callers must reach every collective without exiting.

The addition order is the descending-offset tree: 16, 8, 4, 2, then 1. Each addition combines that lane’s accumulated sum with its partner’s accumulated sum from before the current shuffle; the final shuffle broadcasts lane 0’s result.

To prove exact contribution counts, initially each lane holds only its own input. After offset 16, each representative lane below 16 holds exactly the inputs whose indices equal its lane modulo 16. After each subsequent offset d, every representative lane below d holds exactly the inputs whose indices equal its lane modulo d: its previous set and its partner’s set are disjoint, and their union is precisely that new set. If the partner index is outside 0–18, that partner set is empty, so skipping the addition preserves the invariant. After offset 1, lane 0 therefore contains every input exactly once, and broadcasting it gives that same reduction to every caller.

Every shuffle source is either a valid partner in lanes 0–18 or the caller itself; the broadcast source is lane 0. Every caller’s bit is set, and all nineteen named, non-exited lanes execute the same six shuffle collectives in the same order with the same mask and width—even when they have no partner. The synchronized shuffles provide the required rendezvous; no implicit lockstep execution is assumed.

Use a fold, a 16-lane all-reduce, and an unfold: exactly six float shuffles per caller. The final shuffle uses three different completed sums, not a broadcast from one lane.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ __forceinline__ float sum19(float value)
{
    const unsigned int linear =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned int lane = linear & 31u;
    constexpr unsigned int mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    // Fold inputs 16, 17, 18 into lanes 0, 1, 2.
    const int fold_source = static_cast<int>(
        lane < 3u ? lane + 16u : lane);
    const float extra = __shfl_sync(mask, value, fold_source, 32);
    float sum = value;
    if (lane < 3u) {
        sum = __fadd_rn(sum, extra);
    }

    // All-reduce among lanes 0..15; lanes 16..18 still participate.
    #pragma unroll
    for (unsigned int bit = 1u; bit <= 8u; bit <<= 1) {
        const int source = static_cast<int>(
            lane < 16u ? (lane ^ bit) : lane);
        const float partner = __shfl_sync(mask, sum, source, 32);
        if (lane < 16u) {
            sum = __fadd_rn(sum, partner);
        }
    }

    // Unfold: each extra lane reads its own corresponding representative.
    const int unfold_source = static_cast<int>(
        lane >= 16u ? lane - 16u : lane);
    return __shfl_sync(mask, sum, unfold_source, 32);
}
```

Compile with nvcc for sm_70 or newer. The calling contract is exactly the stated one: physical lanes 0–18 of a full warp each call once, and lanes 19–31 do not call.

Addition order: first compute input[0] + input[16], input[1] + input[17], and input[2] + input[18]. Then, at XOR distances 1, 2, 4, and 8, each lane below 16 adds its previous sum as the left operand and its partner’s previous sum as the right operand. Each addition uses round-to-nearest-even through __fadd_rn; different callers can have different rounded results because their operand orders differ.

Exact-once coverage: after folding, the 16 representatives hold disjoint input sets: {0,16}, {1,17}, {2,18}, and the singletons {3} through {15}. At each XOR stage, partners hold disjoint groups of representatives; combining them doubles the group size. After four stages, every lane 0–15 contains all 16 representative sets, hence all 19 inputs exactly once. Lanes 16–18 then copy the completed sums from lanes 0–2 respectively, without further addition.

Every source participates: folding reads lanes 16–18 or the caller itself; XOR stages read lanes 0–15 or the caller itself; unfolding reads lanes 0–15. All 19 callers execute all six matching shuffles with the same mask and width, including lanes without useful partners. The synchronized shuffles provide the required rendezvous, so no implicit lockstep is assumed.

Only negative cycles reachable from s and able to reach t make this query unbounded. A negative cycle elsewhere does not affect the answer. Here, “route” means a walk, so repeating a relevant negative cycle can drive its cost arbitrarily low.

```python
def classify_root(N, edges, s, t):
    edges = list(edges)
    forward = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    reverse = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    for u, v, w in edges:
        forward[u].append(v)
        reverse[v].append(u)

    def reachable(start, graph):
        seen = [False] * N
        seen[start] = True
        stack = [start]
        while stack:
            u = stack.pop()
            for v in graph[u]:
                if not seen[v]:
                    seen[v] = True
                    stack.append(v)
        return seen

    from_s = reachable(s, forward)
    if not from_s[t]:
        return ('unreachable', None)
    to_t = reachable(t, reverse)

    relevant = [from_s[v] and to_t[v] for v in range(N)]
    K = sum(relevant)
    query_edges = [
        (u, v, w) for u, v, w in edges
        if relevant[u] and relevant[v]
    ]

    dist = [None] * N
    dist[s] = 0
    for _ in range(K - 1):
        next_dist = dist.copy()
        changed = False
        for u, v, w in query_edges:
            if dist[u] is None:
                continue
            candidate = dist[u] + w
            if next_dist[v] is None or candidate < next_dist[v]:
                next_dist[v] = candidate
                changed = True
        dist = next_dist
        if not changed:
            break

    for u, v, w in query_edges:
        if dist[u] is not None and (
            dist[v] is None or dist[u] + w < dist[v]
        ):
            return ('unbounded', None)

    return ('finite', dist[t])
```

Use Python 3; edges contains directed triples `(u, v, weight)`. For example, `classify_root(2, [(0, 1, -3)], 0, 1)` returns `('finite', -3)`. No dependencies are required.

The invariant is that after k synchronous relaxation rounds, `dist[v]` is the minimum cost of an s-to-v walk using at most k edges in the relevant subgraph, or None if none exists. Copying the distances before each round ensures that every relaxation extends a walk from the preceding round by exactly one edge.

Every s-to-t walk stays in this subgraph. If it has no negative cycle, removing repeated-vertex cycles never increases cost, so a minimum-cost walk can be chosen with at most K−1 edges. Conversely, any relevant negative cycle can be reached, repeated arbitrarily often, and followed by a walk to t. An improvement after K−1 rounds detects such a cycle: without one, all minimum distances already have simple-path representatives. If a negative cycle exists, not all its edge inequalities can be satisfied, because summing them would contradict its negative total weight.

Worst-case time is O(N + M + K(N + M′)), hence O(N(N + M)), counting integer operations; memory is O(N + M). Here M′ is the number of relevant edges. Intermediate costs have magnitude at most K times 10¹², so Python integers remain exact; accounting for integer bit costs adds an O(log(N·10¹²)) factor to relaxation arithmetic. The zero-edge walk handles s equal to t, while parallel edges and self-loops are processed normally.

Only negative cycles reachable from s and able to reach t make the answer unbounded. This uses deterministic Bellman–Ford passes, not a heuristic queue algorithm.

