Usage in Python 3: call `classify_root(N, edges, s, t)`, where each edge is `(u, v, integer_weight)` and vertices are zero-based. Parallel edges and self-loops are preserved.

Invariant: after i completed passes, `dist[v]` is the minimum cost of a walk from s to v using at most i edges in the relevant subgraph, or `None` if no such walk exists. Each pass reads only the previous array and writes a copy, so the invariant follows by either retaining a walk or appending one edge. An unchanged pass is a fixed point, making early termination safe.

The final scan tests whether an additional, K-th-edge relaxation can strictly improve a distance after K−1 passes. Without a negative cycle, removing nonnegative cycles leaves a simple walk with at most K−1 edges, so improvement is impossible. Conversely, if no edge improves, all relevant distances satisfy `dist[v] <= dist[u] + w`; summing those inequalities around a negative cycle gives a contradiction. Thus an improvement establishes a relevant negative cycle, which can be repeated arbitrarily often before continuing to t.

When s equals t, the empty walk has cost zero. The result is therefore `('finite', 0)` unless a negative cycle is reachable from s and can return to s, in which case it is unbounded. Unreachable targets return before relaxation. The explicit `dist[u] is None` checks prevent unreachable distances from entering arithmetic; Python integers avoid overflow.

With M edges, worst-case time is O(N + M + K(N + M)), hence O(N(N + M)), including the array copies. Memory is O(N + M). These bounds hold for every allowed input, without probabilistic or queue-runtime assumptions.

The kernel violates the block-barrier invariant: all non-exited threads in the block must reach the same `__syncthreads()` invocation. With 96 threads and `valid = 70`, only 70 reach it. That is invalid synchronization, not a guarantee that every device must hang; successful launch checks do not establish runtime correctness.

Move the barrier outside the conditional. This complete example supports `valid` from 0 through 96 and returns zero for an empty input.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>

__global__ void sum_valid(const float* input, float* output, int valid)
{
    __shared__ float values[96];
    const int tid = static_cast<int>(threadIdx.x);

    if (tid < valid) {
        values[tid] = input[tid];
    }

    __syncthreads();

    if (tid == 0) {
        float sum = 0.0f;
        for (int i = 0; i < valid; ++i) {
            sum += values[i];
        }
        output[0] = sum;
    }
}

// input must contain at least valid floats when valid > 0.
// output must contain at least one float. Both allocations must remain
// valid until the stream completes. input may be null when valid == 0.
cudaError_t launch_sum(const float* input, float* output,
                       int valid, cudaStream_t stream)
{
    if (valid < 0 || valid > 96 || output == nullptr ||
        (valid > 0 && input == nullptr)) {
        return cudaErrorInvalidValue;
    }
    sum_valid<<<1, 96, 0, stream>>>(input, output, valid);
    return cudaGetLastError();
}

#define CHECK(call) do {                                         \
    cudaError_t error = (call);                                   \
    if (error != cudaSuccess) {                                   \
        std::fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", #call,                   \
                     cudaGetErrorString(error));                  \
        return 1;                                                \
    }                                                            \
} while (false)

int main()
{
    float input[96];
    for (float& value : input) value = 1.0f;

    float* device_input = nullptr;
    float* device_output = nullptr;
    cudaStream_t stream;
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreate(&stream));
    CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&device_input),
                     sizeof(input)));
    CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&device_output),
                     sizeof(float)));
    CHECK(cudaMemcpy(device_input, input, sizeof(input),
                     cudaMemcpyHostToDevice));

    for (int valid = 0; valid <= 96; ++valid) {
        CHECK(launch_sum(device_input, device_output, valid, stream));
        // Detect runtime errors and wait before reading the output.
        CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(stream));
        float result = -1.0f;
        CHECK(cudaMemcpy(&result, device_output, sizeof(result),
                         cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));
        if (result != static_cast<float>(valid)) {
            std::fprintf(stderr, "Incorrect result for valid=%d\n", valid);
            return 1;
        }
    }

    CHECK(cudaFree(device_output));
    CHECK(cudaFree(device_input));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(stream));
    std::puts("All valid counts passed.");
    return 0;
}
```

Compile with `nvcc -std=c++17 sum.cu -o sum` and run on a CUDA-capable GPU.

Every one of the 96 threads reaches the unconditional barrier exactly once. For each index below `valid`, its corresponding thread initializes shared memory before that barrier, which makes the write visible to thread zero afterward. Thread zero reads only those initialized entries, in increasing index order; when `valid` is zero, neither input nor shared memory is read, and the output is explicitly zero. No second barrier is needed because no thread subsequently modifies or reuses shared memory.

In the original kernel with `valid = 70`, threads 70–95 fail `tid < valid`, so they skip the input load, shared-memory write, and conditional `__syncthreads()`. They do not write the output. Threads 0–69 enter the conditional barrier; its condition is not uniform across the block, violating the block-barrier contract. This is invalid synchronization, but it does not imply that every device must hang.

Successful immediate launch checks establish neither successful kernel completion nor correct synchronization. Execution is asynchronous, so runtime failures may surface later; even successful stream synchronization does not prove the absence of synchronization bugs.

The replacement already satisfies the required launch and initialization rules:
```cpp
__global__ void sum_valid(const float* input, float* output, int valid)
{
    __shared__ float values[96];
    const int tid = static_cast<int>(threadIdx.x);

    if (tid < valid) {
        values[tid] = input[tid];
    }

    __syncthreads();

    if (tid == 0) {
        float sum = 0.0f;
        for (int i = 0; i < valid; ++i) {
            sum += values[i];
        }
        output[0] = sum;
    }
}
```
For every accepted `valid` from 0 through 96, `launch_sum` executes exactly `sum_valid<<<1, 96, 0, stream>>>(input, output, valid)`—including when `valid` is zero.

For each index `i` that thread zero reads, `0 <= i < valid <= 96`. Thread `i` therefore exists and initializes `values[i]` from `input[i]` before reaching the unconditional barrier. All 96 threads reach that barrier, which makes those writes visible before thread zero reads them. Entries at or above `valid` need not be initialized because they are never read; input must contain at least `valid` floats.

When `valid = 0`, all 96 threads skip the input load but still execute the barrier. Thread zero initializes its local sum to `0.0f`, executes no loop iterations, and writes `output[0] = 0.0f`. No kernel is skipped and no host-side output initialization is needed.

B’s wait references E’s first record—the most recent record when that wait was submitted. Recording E again does not retarget the existing wait. P2 can race with C1 because there is no dependency ordering P2 after C1; the later query reports the second record’s status, not buffer ownership. I’m interpreting “weight” as `cudaStreamWaitEvent` and “save first use” as safe first use.

A permitted execution is:
1. A queues P1, then E’s first record. B queues a wait on that record, then C1.
2. A queues P2, then E’s second record.
3. P1 completes; the first record completes, making both C1 and P2 eligible to execute.
4. P2 starts overwriting the allocation while C1 reads it. A query of E returns `cudaErrorNotReady` because its second record has not completed.

That execution permits conflicting accesses; it does not guarantee any particular corrupted result. The missing ownership edge is C1 completion before P2 starts overwriting.

This complete example uses two slots. Each slot follows producer → ready → consumer → consumed → next producer. First use skips the consumed wait, rather than relying on an unrecorded event. There is no host synchronization between iterations and no device-wide synchronization.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>

#define CHECK(call) do {                                      \
    cudaError_t err = (call);                                  \
    if (err != cudaSuccess) {                                 \
        std::fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", #call,               \
                     cudaGetErrorString(err));                 \
        std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                              \
    }                                                         \
} while (false)

__global__ void produce(int* slot, int value)
{
    slot[0] = value;
}

__global__ void consume(const int* slot, int* result)
{
    result[0] = slot[0];
}

int main()
{
    constexpr int iterations = 8;
    cudaStream_t A, B;
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&A, cudaStreamNonBlocking));
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&B, cudaStreamNonBlocking));

    struct Slot {
        int* data = nullptr;
        cudaEvent_t ready{};
        cudaEvent_t consumed{};
        bool used = false;
    } slots[2];

    for (auto& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&slot.data),
                         sizeof(int)));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.ready,
                                       cudaEventDisableTiming));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.consumed,
                                       cudaEventDisableTiming));
    }

    int* results = nullptr;
    CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&results),
                     iterations * sizeof(int)));

    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        Slot& slot = slots[i % 2];

        // Reclaim this allocation only after its previous reader finishes.
        if (slot.used) {
            CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(A, slot.consumed, 0));
        }

        produce<<<1, 1, 0, A>>>(slot.data, i + 1);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.ready, A));

        // This wait captures the ready record just submitted above.
        CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(B, slot.ready, 0));
        consume<<<1, 1, 0, B>>>(slot.data, results + i);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.consumed, B));
        slot.used = true;
    }

    // Final drain only: no host waits occurred between iterations.
    CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(B));
    CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(A));

    int host_results[iterations];
    CHECK(cudaMemcpy(host_results, results, sizeof(host_results),
                     cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));
    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        if (host_results[i] != i + 1) {
            std::fprintf(stderr, "Mismatch at iteration %d\n", i);
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
    }

    for (auto& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.ready));
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.consumed));
        CHECK(cudaFree(slot.data));
    }
    CHECK(cudaFree(results));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(B));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(A));
    std::puts("All results passed.");
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

Compile with `nvcc -std=c++17 ownership.cu -o ownership` and run on a CUDA-capable GPU.

For each slot, the ready wait prevents its consumer from reading before production completes. Before reuse, A waits on that slot’s previously recorded consumed event, preventing the next producer from overwriting until the previous consumer finishes. Re-recording events is safe here because each corresponding wait is submitted before the next record of that event; existing waits retain their captured record. These dependencies establish ownership without assuming implicit stream dependencies or simultaneous execution.

At repeatable read, both transactions can commit and leave nobody on call: this is write skew. I’m interpreting “uncall” as “on-call.”

Starting with Alice and Bob both on call, this interleaving is permitted:
1. Alice begins; her count query sees 2.
2. Bob begins; his count query also sees 2.
3. Alice updates Alice’s row to off call.
4. Bob updates Bob’s row to off call.
5. Alice commits, then Bob commits. Both rows are now off call.

Updating different rows matters because there is no competing update to the same row: neither transaction encounters the same-row write conflict that would prevent both updates at PostgreSQL repeatable read. Each decision instead depends on data the other transaction changes.

Run this setup once in PostgreSQL:
```sql
CREATE TABLE doctors (
    name text PRIMARY KEY,
    on_call boolean NOT NULL
);
INSERT INTO doctors VALUES ('Alice', true), ('Bob', true);
```

Run Alice’s transaction in one connection:
```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
DO $$
DECLARE
    n bigint;
BEGIN
    SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM doctors WHERE on_call;
    IF n > 1 THEN
        UPDATE doctors SET on_call = false WHERE name = 'Alice';
    END IF;
END
$$;
COMMIT;
```

Run Bob’s transaction concurrently in another connection:
```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
DO $$
DECLARE
    n bigint;
BEGIN
    SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM doctors WHERE on_call;
    IF n > 1 THEN
        UPDATE doctors SET on_call = false WHERE name = 'Bob';
    END IF;
END
$$;
COMMIT;
```

On SQLSTATE `40001`, whether raised during a statement or at commit, discard that attempt and roll back if the connection remains in a transaction. Retry the entire transaction from a new `BEGIN`: repeat the count, recompute the decision, perform any update, and commit. Retrying only the update or commit is incorrect; application code must implement this retry loop.

PostgreSQL serializable isolation detects the conflicting read/write dependencies and prevents both stale decisions from committing. Successful transactions have an equivalent serial ordering: the first may reduce the count from 2 to 1, but the next then sees 1 and does not remove the remaining doctor. Thus at least one stays on call, provided the initial invariant holds and every operation that could remove an on-call doctor follows this protocol.

