For each slot, the enforced chain is producer → ready record → consumer → consumed record → next producer. Every wait is submitted after its intended event record and before that event is re-recorded, so later records cannot change existing waits. Thus the producer owns the slot until production completes, the consumer has read ownership until consumption completes, and only then can the next producer overwrite it. Separate result elements avoid output conflicts; final stream synchronization keeps all allocations alive until their users finish.

At PostgreSQL repeatable read, both transactions can commit and leave nobody on call—a write-skew failure. I’m interpreting “uncall” as “on-call.”

An exact permitted interleaving is:
1. Alice begins at repeatable read and counts 2 on-call doctors.
2. Bob begins at repeatable read and counts 2 on-call doctors, before Alice updates.
3. Alice updates only Alice to off-call. Bob remains on-call in Alice’s snapshot.
4. Bob updates only Bob to off-call. Alice remains on-call in Bob’s snapshot.
5. Alice commits; Bob commits. The committed rows now both say off-call.

Updating different rows matters because there is no same-row write conflict to force an abort. Snapshot isolation protects their individual writes, not the cross-row invariant their count queries checked.

