If no relevant negative cycle exists, removing nonnegative cycles from a walk gives an equally good or better simple path, with at most K−1 edges. Consequently, a strict improvement `dist[u] + w < dist[v]` in the extra scan establishes a relevant negative cycle. Conversely, if a reachable negative cycle exists, all its vertices have finite distances after K−1 passes; if none of its edges were relaxable, summing their inequalities would imply its total weight was nonnegative—a contradiction. Because that cycle can reach t, repeating it makes s-to-t costs arbitrarily negative.

