B’s wait references E’s first record—the latest record when that wait was submitted. Re-recording E does not retarget the existing wait. P2 can race with C1: nothing orders C1’s read before P2’s overwrite. I’m interpreting “save first use” as safe first use.

A permitted execution is:
1. Submit P1, record E, submit B’s wait and C1, then submit P2 and record E again.
2. P1 finishes, writing 1; E’s first record completes, releasing B’s wait.
3. P2 is still pending, so querying E’s second record returns not ready.
4. P2 executes and writes 2 before C1 executes.
5. C1 reads 2 instead of P1’s 1.
This respects both streams’ ordering. The query describes the second event record, not ownership of the allocation.

Use two slots with the dependency chain producer → ready → consumer → consumed → next producer for that slot. First use skips the consumed wait because no previous consumer exists. Compile with `nvcc -std=c++17 slots.cu -o slots` and run `./slots` on a CUDA-capable device.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>

#define CHECK(call) do {                                      \
    cudaError_t err = (call);                                  \
    if (err != cudaSuccess) {                                 \
        std::fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", #call,                \
                     cudaGetErrorString(err));                 \
        std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                              \
    }                                                         \
} while (false)

__global__ void produce(int* slot, int value)
{
    slot[0] = value;
}

__global__ void consume(const int* slot, int* result)
{
    result[0] = slot[0];
}

int main()
{
    constexpr int iterations = 8;
    cudaStream_t A, B;
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&A, cudaStreamNonBlocking));
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&B, cudaStreamNonBlocking));

    struct Slot {
        int* data = nullptr;
        cudaEvent_t ready{};
        cudaEvent_t consumed{};
        bool used = false;
    } slots[2];

    for (auto& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&slot.data),
                         sizeof(int)));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.ready,
                                       cudaEventDisableTiming));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.consumed,
                                       cudaEventDisableTiming));
    }

    int* results = nullptr;
    CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&results),
                     iterations * sizeof(int)));

    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        Slot& slot = slots[i % 2];

        // First use has no previous consumer to wait for.
        if (slot.used) {
            CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(A, slot.consumed, 0));
        }

        produce<<<1, 1, 0, A>>>(slot.data, i + 1);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.ready, A));

        CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(B, slot.ready, 0));
        consume<<<1, 1, 0, B>>>(slot.data, results + i);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.consumed, B));
        slot.used = true;
    }

    // Final drain only: no host synchronization between iterations.
    CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(B));
    CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(A));

    int host_results[iterations];
    CHECK(cudaMemcpy(host_results, results, sizeof(host_results),
                     cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));
    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        if (host_results[i] != i + 1) {
            std::fprintf(stderr, "Mismatch at iteration %d\n", i);
            std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }
    }

    for (auto& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.ready));
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.consumed));
        CHECK(cudaFree(slot.data));
    }
    CHECK(cudaFree(results));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(B));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(A));
    std::puts("All results passed.");
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

For iteration i, B’s ready wait captures the record after producer i, so consumer i cannot read early. Before the slot is reused at i + 2, A’s consumed wait captures the record after consumer i, so the next producer cannot overwrite early. Each wait is submitted before its event is re-recorded; later records cannot change those captured dependencies. Thus ownership passes from producer to consumer and back without conflicting accesses, device-wide synchronization, or host waits between iterations.

PostgreSQL repeatable read permits write skew: both transactions can commit and leave nobody on call. I’m interpreting “uncall” as “on-call.”

Starting with Alice and Bob both on call, this execution is permitted:
1. Alice begins at repeatable read; her count query returns 2.
2. Bob begins at repeatable read; his count query also returns 2.
3. Alice marks Alice off call. Her snapshot now shows only Bob on call.
4. Bob marks Bob off call. His snapshot still shows Alice on call, because Alice’s change is not in his snapshot.
5. Alice commits; the committed state has Alice off, Bob on.
6. Bob commits; the committed state now has both off.

Updating different rows matters because there is no competing update to the same row to trigger PostgreSQL’s repeatable-read concurrent-update protection. Their shared count predicate does not prevent this write skew.

Run this setup once:
```sql
CREATE TABLE doctors (
    name text PRIMARY KEY,
    on_call boolean NOT NULL
);
INSERT INTO doctors VALUES ('Alice', true), ('Bob', true);
```

Run the following transaction in Alice’s session:
```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
DO $$
DECLARE
    n bigint;
BEGIN
    SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM doctors WHERE on_call;
    IF n > 1 THEN
        UPDATE doctors SET on_call = false WHERE name = 'Alice';
    END IF;
END
$$;
COMMIT;
```

Run this in Bob’s session:
```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
DO $$
DECLARE
    n bigint;
BEGIN
    SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM doctors WHERE on_call;
    IF n > 1 THEN
        UPDATE doctors SET on_call = false WHERE name = 'Bob';
    END IF;
END
$$;
COMMIT;
```

If either session receives SQLSTATE `40001`, roll back if necessary and retry its entire transaction from a fresh `BEGIN`: repeat the count, reevaluate the condition, perform any resulting update, and attempt `COMMIT`. Do not retry only the update or commit, and do not reuse the earlier count. A serialization failure can occur during a statement or at commit; the application must handle both.

Correctness follows from serializability: committed transactions must be equivalent to some serial execution. In either serial order, the first transaction can reduce the count from two to one, but the second then sees one and cannot remove the last on-call doctor. PostgreSQL must therefore reject at least one transaction in the conflicting two-counts-of-two execution. This preserves the invariant provided every operation that could remove an on-call doctor follows this protocol; retries may affect progress, but not the safety of committed results.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ __forceinline__ float sum19(float value)
{
    const unsigned int linear =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned int lane = linear & 31u;
    constexpr unsigned int mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    float sum = value;
    #pragma unroll
    for (int offset = 16; offset >= 1; offset >>= 1) {
        const bool has_partner =
            lane < static_cast<unsigned int>(offset) &&
            lane + static_cast<unsigned int>(offset) < 19u;
        const int source = has_partner
            ? static_cast<int>(lane) + offset
            : static_cast<int>(lane);

        const float partner = __shfl_sync(mask, sum, source, 32);
        if (has_partner) {
            sum = sum + partner;
        }
    }

    return __shfl_sync(mask, sum, 0, 32);
}
```
